y that  ukraine talks about um providing the the  eu with power over winter um do we  presume this not to be a coincidence  um  no i think they've been trying for that  power station for a while it's been long  contested i think russia managed to box  the way around it um  it's interesting it is the most  significant non-nuclear power station in  ukraine but it has routinely been out of  operation due to its position in the  donbass uh and on the front lines um  and interestingly right at the start of  the war and this was already scheduled  but ukraine actually switched its grid  towards pointing towards europe whereas  privacy is pointing towards russia so um  the value of that power station actually  to to ukraine has certainly fallen off  um because they're now able to go on to  the european grids and and that's how  they've been able to make good the loss  of not only that station but more  importantly zapariza  nuclear power plant which is the biggest  in europe probably about twice the power  output of uh this older station in the  donbass that's been captured now so it's  sort of symbolically important but i'd  agree with you create an assessment but  it's it's a tactical game for russia  it's not a a big strategic thing at this  point  out of ukraine itself the the uk has  started several weeks of training for  the first cohort of up to ten thousand  ukrainians they say um is that likely to  to make a difference i i i wonder about  that the um why you would take them out  of the country to to do this rather than  than have them there and on hand  you know it's a very important point  because  it's not just about donating equipment  you've got to train people how to use it  and particularly as you talk about  ukrainian counter-attack um to do that  at a higher level actually we've seen  anyone operate in the conflict in terms  of using your troops and your equipment  together to affect rather than piecemeal  attacks  that we've maybe seen from russia and  th